WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.350
Hey, this is kind of interesting and I think
it's a good opportunity for an important discussion.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.420
MSNBC host Joy and Reid, Joy Reid, I guess
she goes by now.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:15.360
On Tuesday, she was accused of Islamophobia
over some remarks that she made on the Monday

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:22.621
evening program where she kind of equated
radicalized Trump supporters to, quote, the

00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:24.180
way Muslims act.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.250
And, of course, that doesn't sound good.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.510
And she is rightly being criticized.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:32.910
And I think this was actually a missed opportunity
to bring something to the forefront.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.220
That is a really important discussion to have.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:43.550
So the setup is that they were having a panel
on her program about President Trump not denouncing

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:51.590
his supporters role in the violence in in
being violent at protests in Kenosha and in

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:52.590
Portland.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:59.780
And after noting how Trump liked a tweet praising
vigilante Kyle Rittenhouse and cheering pro

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.950
Trump Trump demonstrators in Portland.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.310
Reid tried to make a point about the president
radicalizing his base.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.830
And it's sounding like the way Muslims act.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.440
It doesn't sound good and it wasn't good.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.490
Take a look at the video.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:20.470
It went one thing when leaders, let's say,
in the Muslim world, talk a lot of violent

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:26.900
talk and encourage their supporters to be
willing to commit violence, including on their

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.520
own bodies, in order to win against whoever
they decide is the enemy.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.840
We in the U.S. media describe that as they
are radicalizing those people, particularly

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.270
in their radicalizing young people.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:43.890
That's how we talk about the way Muslims act
when you see what Donald Trump is doing.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:49.810
Is that any different from what we describe
as radicalizing people?

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.240
I don't believe so.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.200
So, Joy, Reid is being rightly criticized
here because she's saying, oh, so all Muslims

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.200
are radical.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:05.009
She's using language where she says the most
radical Trump ists are like Muslims generally.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:08.229
And of course, that couldn't be further from
the truth.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:10.189
We've got over a million Muslims.

00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:14.510
The overwhelming majority, as we all know,
are not radicals and they're not violent.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.150
And all of that stuff.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.930
But there was really an opportunity here to
have an important conversation, the conversation

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.030
to be had.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.370
And I would have been with only a slight change
to wording Joy.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.629
Reid would have been able to do it.

00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:32.609
The conversation is extremism starts to look
the same.

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:37.829
Any time religious fanaticism or cult like
behavior is at play.

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:43.309
And when you look at right wing Christians
and the most extreme movements within right

00:02:43.309 --> 00:02:49.420
wing Christianity, when you look at radical
Islamists, when you look at Trump cultists

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.659
with guns, they are all very similar.

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:59.559
The cult like reverence to whatever, sometimes
its God, sometimes it's a religious leader,

00:02:59.559 --> 00:03:00.670
sometimes it's a cult leader.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.669
Sometimes it's in the case of Trump, an elected
official.

00:03:03.669 --> 00:03:05.370
That is a real consideration.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:10.249
And in fact, it's part of the conversation
where when we talk about political violence,

00:03:10.249 --> 00:03:15.579
you often see it categorized as right wing
violence, including stuff like militias and

00:03:15.579 --> 00:03:22.120
sovereign citizens and white nationalists,
eco terrorism like, you know, the small slice

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:27.129
of political violence that would be based
on things like climate defense on the left,

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:31.010
very rare, and then Islamic political violence.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:36.810
I've made the case for years that if you believe
religion should be the supreme law of the

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:41.810
land and you are willing to go and commit
violence in the name of religion, it doesn't

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.760
really matter what religion that is.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.389
That's almost certainly right wing violence.

00:03:46.389 --> 00:03:51.799
So I've been saying for a while, radical Islamic
violence is right wing.

00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:56.499
And when you look at the precepts and the
beliefs of radical Islamists and compare them

00:03:56.499 --> 00:04:01.029
to radical Christians and white nationalists
who are often influenced by Christianity,

00:04:01.029 --> 00:04:03.900
they're really almost the same.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.689
Different details internally and they're really
all right wing.

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:10.739
So I get why Joy Reid is being criticized.

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:12.670
She didn't address this the right way.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.699
She simply said this is how Muslims Act, which
sounds like she's saying this is how all Muslims

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:17.699
act.

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:21.459
And it's important to remember that when we
on the left talk about this.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:26.419
I never say all Republicans x all Trump ists.

00:04:26.419 --> 00:04:30.740
Why we don't say all Muslims X, of course.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:38.319
And the missed opportunity here would be to
say what we are talking about here is that

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:41.199
radicalism all starts to look the same.

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:46.770
We should be able to apply the same language
to it when we talk about Trump, radicals,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.199
Muslim radicals or whoever.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:55.129
Congresswoman Ellen Omar tweeted about this
saying, quote, Honestly, this kind of casual

00:04:55.129 --> 00:04:57.360
Islamophobia is hurtful and dangerous.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.460
We deserve better.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.729
And an apology for the painful moment for
so many Muslims around our country should

00:05:02.729 --> 00:05:03.729
be forthcoming.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:08.169
It is hurtful and it's unproductive, but it
should be OK to talk about how all extremism

00:05:08.169 --> 00:05:12.370
starts to look very similar in a way that's
serious and in a way that sober, sober.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:19.889
So Joy Reid missed an opportunity to say these
radical trumpet's show the same type of radicalization

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:25.479
that we see in all radicals, including radicalized
Muslims, who these radical trumpet's claim

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:26.620
to oppose.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.560
They are more similar, in fact, than they
would have you believe.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.349
That has to be OK to talk about.

00:05:32.349 --> 00:05:36.599
I believe that if Joy Reid had couched it
in those terms, it would not have gotten the

00:05:36.599 --> 00:05:37.849
backlash that it received.

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:40.060
A radical is a radical.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:44.969
Doesn't matter if it's a you know, based on
race, color, creed, politics, a radical is

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:47.910
radical and it all starts to look the same.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:48.910
Let me know what you think.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.939
I'm on Twitter at David Pakman.

